/P 607 0 R
/E (1. Bear Market Phase: Following the peak and correction of a bull market, the market enters a bear market phase. Prices start declining, and investor sentiment becomes pessimistic. The bear market can last for an extended period, usually lasting for several months to a year or more.)
/Pg 31 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
974 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 607 0 R
/E (2. Accumulation Phase: After the end of the previous bear market, the crypto market enters an accumulation phase, where prices are relatively low and investor interest is limited. Savvy investors, traders, and institutions started accumulating cryptocurrencies during this period as they believed prices would rise again.)
/Pg 31 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
975 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 607 0 R
/E (3. Bitcoin Halving Cycle: The BTC halving cycle is a key part of the four-year crypto market cycle. It happens approximately every four years, reducing the block reward for miners. This event often precedes a bull market phase, with reduced supply driving increased demand and price growth. While the halving plays a significant role, other factors like adoption and regulation also influence the market.)
/Pg 31 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
976 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 607 0 R
/E (4. PARABOLIC Bull Market Phase: As the accumulation phase progresses, positive news, increased adoption, and growing interest drive demand for cryptocurrencies. This results in a significant upward trend in prices, leading to a bull market. During this phase, prices of cryptocurrencies, especially Bitcoin, can experience substantial gains, often reaching new all-time highs.)
/Pg 31 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
977 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 607 0 R
